[{:seon.task/name "the-branch-roster-is-compacted"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: the branch roster is a konserve append log (`datahike.roster`), one entry a `branch!` or delete, never compacted. ``d/branches`` reads the whole log, one konserve read an entry (11.7k ticks and 33 KB each, measured 2026-11-11), deleted branches included: 94k ticks at 1 branch, 1.25 M at 100, 11.8 M and 34 MB at 1,000. A root's store gains a branch an agent, so the log only grows. Correct: the log is folded into one value when it is read past a bound, or a delete rewrites it, so ``d/branches`` costs the live branches (one read when compact). Upstream's own versioning first (`branch-history`, durable GC roots): take its shape if it has one. Proof when built: ``d/branches`` after n branches made and deleted costs what the live ones cost. Absorbed in curation 2026-11-14 (same subject and evidence): seon.db/a-long-running-store-stays-bounded (A user's store runs for months with no one erasing it)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-commits-changes-are-kept-and-read-in-their-size"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: deps; first caller data-2's merge (landing 3), then runtime-2's advance!, context-4's page diffs, the vector round. Agreed by root 2026-11-15 (deps-collapse-plan-2026-11-15-0756.md); context, runtime and web each reached \"one diff per commit\" on their own.\n\nWhat exists [read]: Datahike knows which tx ids a commit added (datahike.versioning/commit-tx-range) and walks the commit graph with a per-process memory (commit-at-tx), but keeps no datoms per transaction, so every \"what changed between X and Y\" diffs whole index values or reads d/as-of views. Measured [M, 2026-11-15]: seon.merge/own-datoms on lb76's change (4,849 added, 168 retracted) ~3.1B ticks, 30x the apply (119M); datoms-diff at d = 7,163 139M ticks and 429 MB (~60 KB a datom); context's page diffs ~150M ticks each over 128 turn commits. About 20 Seon functions compute such a diff each their own way (seon.merge/own-datoms and changes, seon.agent/main-changes and branch-changes, seon.ctx/changes, seon.ctx.wire/page-diff-msgs, seon.vector's round, seon.program/diff-plan and load-changes!, seon.problems/changed-paths).\n\nDesign (the field's way: Datomic's log and tx-range): the writer's commit loop passes the group's effective tx-data on the committed db's meta (as :datahike/merge-parents travels, writing.cljc merge-writer-over); datahike.writing/commit! writes it once, beside the commit record, under a key of the commit id, before the head flips and inside the same collection guard; collection drops it with its commit. (d/changes conn-or-db from-commit to-commit) walks the first-parent chain from to back to from (commit-at-tx's memory) and concatenates the commits' changes in order; a fact added and retracted within the range nets out by [e a v]. A merge commit's changes are its own new transactions, as commit-tx-range already defines. A commit written before this has none: d/changes answers a typed :datahike/changes-unrecorded naming it, and the caller's one fallback is today's diff until the store is rebuilt.\n\nEstimate (lower bound, written first): write, one konserve value a commit, O(d) bytes, ~50-80 B a datom in fressian [H, measured first on a real commit's tx-data before any code]; on a 1-datom commit about one extra key write (~10 us). Read, for k commits and d datoms: k gets (~10-30 us each, LMDB) + decoding d datoms (~1 us each). lb76's merge, k ~900 commits and d ~5k: ~30 ms and ~0.5 MB against ~3.1B ticks (seconds) and hundreds of MB today, ~100x less. Store growth: +1x the datoms each commit writes, against an index that already keeps every datom in its current or temporal tree.\n\nDeletes, in the callers' landings: each caller's own diff (the list above), each named in its landing's Deletes:. No functionality removed: each caller keeps its answer, from the changes.\n\nProof, red first: datahike.versioning-test/a-commits-changes-are-read-in-their-size: two branches of one store, n small commits on one; d/changes from the fork point to its head equals its net datoms by [e a v] and reads only those commits (its ticks flat in the store's size, measured at two store sizes); a merge commit's changes are its own transactions; a pre-existing commit answers :datahike/changes-unrecorded."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{datahike.versioning-test/a-commits-changes-are-read-in-their-size}}
]
